{%- comment -%}
  Size, colourway, quantity, add to bag — port of
  components/product/product-purchase.tsx.

  Shopify holds a variant per combination of option values, so the option grids
  resolve to a single variant id, which is the only thing the form posts. Price,
  stock and availability are all read from that variant and never computed here —
  the same rule the React version worked to, except that here the variant is
  Shopify's own object rather than one found in a fixture.

  THE ONE ARCHITECTURAL CHANGE, and it is an improvement. React held the
  selection in useState, so the picker did not work without JavaScript. Here each
  option value is a LINK to ?variant=<id> — the same "URL is the state" rule the
  catalogue filters follow. That makes the picker work with script off, makes a
  chosen variant shareable, and makes the back button undo exactly one choice.
  Shopify resolves `product.selected_or_first_available_variant` from that param,
  so there is no state to keep in step.

  A combination with no stock does not silently do nothing: the value is rendered
  disabled and struck through, and where the pair itself is the problem the action
  is disabled and says so in words. Sold out is never communicated by colour
  alone.

  THREE ROUTES OUT OF THIS BLOCK, and they are deliberately not three buttons.

  `Add to bag` and `Buy now` are the brief's two CTAs and they are the same POST
  to /cart/add — `Buy now` is a second submit carrying `name="return_to"
  value="/checkout"`, so only the button the shopper actually pressed adds that
  parameter. Exactly the pattern cart-lines.liquid uses for its two quantities.
  It is NOT Shopify's dynamic checkout button: `{{ form | payment_button }}`
  needs Shopify Payments, which does not exist in Indonesia (see CLAUDE.md,
  Platform constraints). So `Buy now` reaches checkout through the cart in one
  silent hop rather than accelerating past it, which is the whole difference.

  `Buy on Shopee` is the third route and it is a LINK, not a button. It is a
  channel choice rather than a purchase intent — the same piece, bought
  somewhere else — and giving it a third button would put the marketplace at
  parity with the store's own checkout on the store's own product page. It stays
  visible on a sold-out product on purpose: this catalogue's stock data came
  from Shopee, but that was a snapshot, and hiding the link would assert a
  restock has not happened when nothing here knows that.

  The link renders only where `custom.shopee_url` is set, falling back to the
  shop-wide URL in theme settings. With neither it is absent — a marketplace
  button that lands on a search page is the dead link this repo refuses.

  Accepts: product
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign current = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  unless current
    assign current = product.variants.first
  endunless
  assign purchasable = false
  if current and current.available
    assign purchasable = true
  endif

  assign shopee_url = product.metafields.custom.shopee_url.value | default: settings.shopee_shop_url

  # `render` takes a variable or a literal, never an expression — writing
  # `disabled: purchasable == false` is a parse error, not a falsy value. The
  # negation has to exist as its own variable before the two calls below can
  # use it. (And inside a liquid tag a comment is a `#` line, not a comment
  # block — a stray percent-brace in here closes the tag early.)
  assign unavailable = true
  if purchasable
    assign unavailable = false
  endif
-%}

<form method="post" action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}" id="options" class="flex flex-col gap-5.5">
  <input type="hidden" name="form_type" value="product">
  <input type="hidden" name="utf8" value="✓">
  <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ current.id }}">

  {%- comment -%}
    A product with no real options still reports one — Shopify's synthetic
    "Title" option, whose single value is "Default Title". Rendering it draws a
    fieldset labelled Title holding one chip that says Default Title, which is
    an option the shopper does not have. Every piece imported from the Shopee
    catalogue is in that state, so the picker has to sit behind this guard.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- unless product.has_only_default_variant -%}
  {%- for option in product.options_with_values -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign option_index = forloop.index
      assign lowered = option.name | downcase
      assign is_colour = false
      if lowered contains 'colour' or lowered contains 'color'
        assign is_colour = true
      endif
      assign selected_value = current.options[forloop.index0]
    -%}

    <fieldset
      data-variant-field
      class="flex flex-col gap-3 {% if forloop.first %}border-t border-hairline pt-5.5{% endif %}"
    >
      {%- if is_colour -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The chosen colourway gets its own line at h3, not a 14px caption tucked
          beside the label: the reviews argue about colour BY NAME, so the name is
          what the shopper is reading, and a 40px disc cannot say "Espresso". No
          `font-display` — base.css sets h3 and below in the interface face. The
          line is height-held so swapping variants never shifts the swatch row.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
          <legend class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">{{ option.name }}</legend>
          <span class="min-h-7 text-h3 leading-h3 text-ink">{{ selected_value }}</span>
        </div>
      {%- else -%}
        <legend class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">{{ option.name }}</legend>
      {%- endif -%}

      <div class="flex flex-wrap {% if is_colour %}gap-3.5{% else %}gap-2.5{% endif %}">
        {%- for value in option.values -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The variant this value would land on: the current selection with just
            this one option swapped. Nothing is guessed — if no such variant
            exists the value is rendered unavailable rather than linked.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign target = nil
            for candidate in product.variants
              assign matches = true
              for other in product.options_with_values
                assign other_index = forloop.index
                if other_index == option_index
                  if candidate.options[forloop.index0] != value
                    assign matches = false
                  endif
                else
                  if candidate.options[forloop.index0] != current.options[forloop.index0]
                    assign matches = false
                  endif
                endif
              endfor
              if matches
                assign target = candidate
                break
              endif
            endfor

            assign is_selected = false
            if value == selected_value
              assign is_selected = true
            endif

            assign is_available = false
            if target and target.available
              assign is_available = true
            endif
          -%}

          {%- if is_colour -%}
            {%- assign swatch = value.swatch.color -%}
            {%- if is_available -%}
              <a
                href="{{ product.url }}?variant={{ target.id }}"
                data-variant-link
                {% if is_selected %}aria-current="true"{% endif %}
                title="{{ value }}"
                {% if swatch %}style="background-color:{{ swatch }}"{% endif %}
                class="wl-tap size-10 rounded-pill border border-line transition-shadow duration-(--duration-base) {% if is_selected %}ring-2 ring-brand ring-offset-2{% else %}hover:ring-1 hover:ring-line{% endif %} {% unless swatch %}bg-tone{% endunless %}"
              ><span class="sr-only">{{ value }}</span></a>
            {%- else -%}
              <span
                aria-disabled="true"
                title="{{ value }}"
                {% if swatch %}style="background-color:{{ swatch }}"{% endif %}
                class="wl-tap inline-block size-10 rounded-pill border border-line opacity-40 {% unless swatch %}bg-tone{% endunless %}"
              ><span class="sr-only">{{ value }} — {{ 'product.sold_out' | t }}</span></span>
            {%- endif -%}

          {%- else -%}
            {%- if is_available -%}
              <a
                href="{{ product.url }}?variant={{ target.id }}"
                data-variant-link
                {% if is_selected %}aria-current="true"{% endif %}
                class="wl-press-soft inline-flex h-11.5 min-w-13.5 items-center justify-center rounded-xs border px-3 text-caption tracking-wide transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) {% if is_selected %}border-brand bg-brand text-on-brand{% else %}border-line bg-canvas text-ink-body hover:border-brand hover:text-brand{% endif %}"
              >{{ value }}</a>
            {%- else -%}
              <span
                aria-disabled="true"
                class="inline-flex h-11.5 min-w-13.5 items-center justify-center rounded-xs border border-inert-border bg-inert px-3 text-caption tracking-wide text-on-inert line-through"
              >{{ value }}<span class="sr-only"> — {{ 'product.sold_out' | t }}</span></span>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    </fieldset>
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endunless -%}

  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-stretch gap-3.5">
    {%- comment -%}
      The stepper's two buttons are an enhancement; the number input underneath
      them is what actually carries the value, so quantity still works with
      script off. That is why they are type="button" and not submits.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="inline-flex items-center rounded-xs border border-line bg-canvas" data-stepper>
      <button
        type="button"
        data-stepper-down
        class="h-14 w-12 cursor-pointer text-h3 text-brand transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) hover:bg-surface-muted disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:text-on-inert"
      >−<span class="sr-only">{{ 'product.decrease' | t }}</span></button>

      <input
        type="number"
        name="quantity"
        value="1"
        min="1"
        max="10"
        step="1"
        aria-label="{{ 'product.quantity' | t }}"
        data-numeric
        data-stepper-value
        class="w-13 border-0 bg-transparent text-center text-body text-ink [appearance:textfield] focus:outline-none [&::-webkit-inner-spin-button]:appearance-none [&::-webkit-outer-spin-button]:appearance-none"
      >

      <button
        type="button"
        data-stepper-up
        class="h-14 w-12 cursor-pointer text-h3 text-brand transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) hover:bg-surface-muted disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:text-on-inert"
      >+<span class="sr-only">{{ 'product.increase' | t }}</span></button>
    </div>

    {%- assign add_label = 'product.add_to_bag' | t -%}
    {% render 'button',
      label: add_label,
      variant: 'primary',
      type: 'submit',
      disabled: unavailable,
      class: 'flex-1 px-6 sm:px-11' %}

    {%- comment -%}
      `class` here REPLACES save-button's own default, which is where its press
      classes live — so they have to be restated or this is the one control on the
      site that does not answer a tap. Painted 56px, so wl-press-soft's 0.98 is
      the right depth; wl-tap comes along because it is what the default carries
      and it is a no-op above 44px.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'save-button', product: product, class: 'wl-tap wl-press-soft size-14 rounded-xs border border-ink-subtle hover:bg-inert' %}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    `Buy now` takes its own full-width row rather than sharing one with the bag.
    Side by side at 0.22em tracking the pair needs about 430px and the purchase
    column is 390px minus the gutter on a phone, so they would wrap anyway — and
    a wrapped pair puts the secondary action level with the stepper, where it
    reads as part of the quantity control.

    It carries the same `purchasable` guard as the bag. A checkout button that
    posts a sold-out variant fails at Shopify's end with an error the shopper
    has to read to understand, which is worse than a control that says up front
    it cannot be used.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign buy_label = 'product.buy_now' | t -%}
  {% render 'button',
    label: buy_label,
    variant: 'secondary',
    type: 'submit',
    name: 'return_to',
    value: '/checkout',
    disabled: unavailable,
    class: 'w-full px-6 sm:px-11' %}

  {%- comment -%}
    Present from first render, so a message is an update to a region that already
    exists rather than a new one appearing.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <p aria-live="polite" class="min-h-5 text-caption text-ink-muted">
    {%- if purchasable -%}
      {{ form.errors | default_errors }}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ 'product.unavailable_variant' | t }}
    {%- endif -%}
  </p>

  {%- if shopee_url != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      An off-site destination, so it says so: `target="_blank"` keeps the
      shopper's place on this page, `rel="noopener"` is required with it, and the
      screen-reader-only suffix is what stops "Buy on Shopee" from being a link
      that silently replaces the page for someone who cannot see the new tab
      open. `noreferrer` is deliberately NOT here — Shopee is the studio's own
      shop and the referrer is how their analytics attribute a visit the site
      sent them.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a
      href="{{ shopee_url }}"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-1.5 self-start text-caption uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-muted transition-colors duration-(--duration-base) hover:text-brand"
    >
      {{ 'product.buy_on_shopee' | t }}
      {% render 'icon', icon: 'arrow-right', class: 'size-4' %}
      <span class="sr-only">{{ 'general.opens_new_tab' | t }}</span>
    </a>
  {%- endif -%}
</form>
